1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The fearsome arsenal wielded by history's outcasts.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Deceptively simple and ingeniously lethal.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Weapons that stabbed, slashed, terrorized,

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
and challenged a mighty empire.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
It's time to probe their intriguing secrets.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Barbarian Battletech on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
For hundreds of years before Christ, and hundreds more after,

8
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,060
Europe's indigenous tribes did battle.

9
00:00:49,061 --> 00:00:52,320
Their world was harsh and savage, in which survival was

10
00:00:52,321 --> 00:00:57,760
won by those with the deadliest weapons and the will to use them.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Their tribal names exude a certain swagger to this very day,

12
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:10,440
the Huns, Vandals, Goths, Saxons, Celts, and Franks.

13
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:21,260
The Greeks and Romans branded them barbarians,

14
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:25,360
considering them uncivilized, vile, more animal than human.

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,500
The term barbarian originally comes from the Greek.

16
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:37,800
And it's what the Greeks called everybody

17
00:01:37,801 --> 00:01:39,100
who wasn't a Greek speaker.

18
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:42,540
The Romans take it up, and they refer it to everybody

19
00:01:42,541 --> 00:01:46,060
that they considered to be non-Roman outside the empire.

20
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:49,940
People themselves certainly didn't call themselves barbarians.

21
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,600
The so-called barbarians lived during the Iron Age,

22
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:57,240
an era of war and emerging technologies,

23
00:01:57,241 --> 00:02:00,340
when metalsmiths forged the weapons that shaped Europe.

24
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:05,440
No matter when man has fought,

25
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:07,280
there have been technological devices

26
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:10,280
that he's used to increase his ability to fight.

27
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:12,280
A club is better than the hands.

28
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:14,120
A sword is better than a club.

29
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:15,820
And in the case of the barbarians,

30
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:19,160
we have actually very strong technology.

31
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:25,400
The British Museum in London

32
00:02:25,401 --> 00:02:28,700
plays host to barbarian weapons once wielded in the fury of

33
00:02:28,701 --> 00:02:29,700
combat.

34
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:39,900
Among them is the 2,300-year-old Kirkburn sword.

35
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:46,900
The sword was unearthed in 1987 from a gravesite in Kirkburn,

36
00:02:46,901 --> 00:02:49,900
Yorkshire. Its hilt was made of bronze,

37
00:02:49,901 --> 00:02:54,400
animal horn, and red glass. Its blade, nearly three feet long,

38
00:02:54,401 --> 00:02:57,240
was forged from the metal that changed the face of ancient

39
00:02:57,340 --> 00:02:59,740
combat, iron.

40
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,840
It was crafted, and no doubt used with lethal efficiency,

41
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:11,760
by one of history's most feared barbarian tribes, the Celts.

42
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:20,160
In 800 BC, the Celts dominated much of Europe.

43
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:24,400
By the first century, they had settled in Britain.

44
00:03:24,401 --> 00:03:26,700
The Celts are a very obscure people

45
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,000
because they date from pre-Roman times

46
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:31,200
when we don't have historical records.

47
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:34,840
So essentially, we know about them from archaeology.

48
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:39,740
Archaeologists have discovered Celtic artifacts,

49
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:42,860
showcasing the mastery of the culture's weapons makers.

50
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:51,760
Hector Cole is a world-renowned ironsmith,

51
00:03:51,761 --> 00:03:54,560
skilled in the traditional methods used by Celtic sword

52
00:03:54,561 --> 00:04:00,560
makers. Like his brethren before him,

53
00:04:00,561 --> 00:04:03,600
he relies on instinct as much as science.

54
00:04:03,601 --> 00:04:08,000
When you put your metal in the fire, your mind goes in the

55
00:04:08,001 --> 00:04:12,000
fire with it. If it doesn't, then you will lose

56
00:04:12,001 --> 00:04:16,000
control of it. And a couple of degrees too high, you will burn

57
00:04:16,001 --> 00:04:20,000
the metal, and it's no good. A few degrees below, and the

58
00:04:20,001 --> 00:04:22,640
and the pieces of metal will not hammer together

59
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:25,040
and become one.

60
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:29,940
The earliest Celt smiths forged weapons from bronze.

61
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:33,640
By the 5th century BC, they were fashioning harder, stronger,

62
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:37,560
deadlier arms, like the Kirkburn sword from iron.

63
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:43,560
Carefully working fire and bellows,

64
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:46,960
Hector has heated a chunk of iron ore, extracting the iron

65
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:50,000
into a catch basin below.

66
00:04:50,001 --> 00:04:53,700
He then molded the iron to form three sword-length billets.

67
00:04:53,701 --> 00:04:56,040
Hector now begins to fuse the billets together

68
00:04:56,041 --> 00:04:57,040
to make a blade.

69
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:06,240
I've taken the rocks from the ground

70
00:05:06,241 --> 00:05:09,660
and heated them and made them into iron.

71
00:05:09,661 --> 00:05:11,760
That in itself is magic.

72
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:16,000
So you can see how these swords built up a reputation

73
00:05:16,001 --> 00:05:17,900
through their mysticism.

74
00:05:17,901 --> 00:05:22,200
And if it was a good sword, a good sword was prized above

75
00:05:22,201 --> 00:05:23,940
anything else.

76
00:05:23,941 --> 00:05:30,540
So carrying on, you can see now it's starting to take shape.

77
00:05:30,541 --> 00:05:32,840
The charcoal in which the iron billets heat up

78
00:05:32,841 --> 00:05:34,760
contains traces of carbon.

79
00:05:34,761 --> 00:05:36,660
The carbon acts as a hardening agent,

80
00:05:36,661 --> 00:05:41,060
and by pounding it into the iron, Hector produces steel.

81
00:05:41,061 --> 00:05:43,460
Up to a limit, the higher the carbon content,

82
00:05:43,461 --> 00:05:44,560
the stronger the steel.

83
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:51,040
After the shaft cools, Hector hammers its sides,

84
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:52,000
compacting the metal.

85
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:55,380
The result is the kind of hard, powerful steel edge

86
00:05:55,381 --> 00:05:58,700
that bloodied and killed countless Celtic foes.

87
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:05,940
The Celtic sword was, on the whole, a slashing weapon

88
00:06:05,941 --> 00:06:08,600
so that you were cutting.

89
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:12,180
You didn't want to be sticking it into your opponent.

90
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:16,220
You were battering them down, battering their shield down,

91
00:06:16,221 --> 00:06:19,580
and then slicing into them to immobilize them.

92
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:29,080
Finally, Hector forges a tongue

93
00:06:29,081 --> 00:06:32,680
where an ornamental handle will slide atop the cold, hard steel

94
00:06:32,681 --> 00:06:35,100
of the Celts' most prized killing tool.

95
00:06:37,180 --> 00:06:39,940
The Celtic sword was about 30 inches long.

96
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:44,640
Her rival weapon, the Roman gladius, was similar in length,

97
00:06:44,641 --> 00:06:48,180
but had a sharp-edged blade with a short pointed tip designed

98
00:06:48,181 --> 00:06:52,440
for stabbing. The Celtic sword featured a heavier blade,

99
00:06:52,441 --> 00:06:57,780
more effective for slashing. 60 hours of hard hammering,

100
00:06:57,781 --> 00:07:02,800
forging away, grinding, polishing, and getting a handle put on it,

101
00:07:02,801 --> 00:07:09,800
we now have a Celtic sword that is fit to give to any warrior

102
00:07:09,801 --> 00:07:11,300
to take into battle.

103
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:19,640
The Celts augmented their masterful weaponry with a unique

104
00:07:19,641 --> 00:07:23,640
system of defense. They devised ingenious fortified

105
00:07:23,641 --> 00:07:26,640
dwellings, many of which still slice into the English

106
00:07:26,641 --> 00:07:31,640
countryside. In Oswestry, England, lie the

107
00:07:31,641 --> 00:07:36,640
contours of a 2,600-year-old Celtic hill fort, a far cry

108
00:07:36,641 --> 00:07:41,640
from the era's classic square structures with towering walls.

109
00:07:41,641 --> 00:07:45,680
Seizing the high ground, the Celts used the natural topography

110
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:48,040
of the land to their advantage.

111
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:51,380
We can see that the Celts chose this spot for the views,

112
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:55,160
the fact that you can see all around of anybody coming to

113
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:56,260
attack.

114
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:59,860
And they have built a series of banks going all the way up

115
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:02,200
to the top.

116
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:04,760
Oswestry was fortified around its perimeter

117
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,760
with a series of earthen barriers.

118
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:10,760
The lower mounds were crowned with a wooden palisade,

119
00:08:10,761 --> 00:08:14,760
The upper mounds were topped with stone walls.

120
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:18,040
Atop the hill was a clearing where hundreds of Celts lived

121
00:08:18,140 --> 00:08:20,500
in thatched roof homes.

122
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:23,500
Not only have we got these multiple banks and ditches

123
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:25,700
coming up through, but we've also

124
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:31,480
got a bank which runs along the entrance on both sides of it

125
00:08:31,580 --> 00:08:35,860
so that it cuts off anybody being able to get into the ditches,

126
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:38,860
up onto the top of the banks, and attack the hill fort

127
00:08:38,861 --> 00:08:41,400
in that way.

128
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:46,100
So they have to come along that track down there.

129
00:08:46,200 --> 00:08:48,600
Invaders who dared to attack through the front entrance

130
00:08:48,700 --> 00:08:52,400
soon became trapped in a two-sided free fire killing

131
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:53,500
zone.

132
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:56,520
Those fortunate enough to battle their way through faced a

133
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:58,420
fortified gate at the top.

134
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:01,620
On each side of the gate waited yet another obstacle.

135
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:05,160
If you look at the bank in front of us here,

136
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:08,820
it is still very steep, quite high, very steep,

137
00:09:08,860 --> 00:09:11,800
If you're attacking, then it's a considerable slope.

138
00:09:11,860 --> 00:09:14,600
You've got the stone wall on the top, possibly timber,

139
00:09:14,660 --> 00:09:17,500
but also you're going to have people up there throwing things

140
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:22,100
at you, and that really does make it almost impossible to get

141
00:09:22,180 --> 00:09:24,100
up to the top.

142
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:27,020
Yet despite the several layers of protection,

143
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:29,320
Celtic hill forts were not impenetrable.

144
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:36,080
In the early 1st century, the Roman army descended on the

145
00:09:36,081 --> 00:09:39,080
on the Celts and Britain.

146
00:09:39,180 --> 00:09:43,620
In 43 AD, the Romans invade Britain under Claudius,

147
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:51,220
the Emperor Claudius, and they face substantial opposition,

148
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:55,440
but disunited opposition because the Celtic tribes were not a

149
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:58,840
single unit.

150
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:02,040
The Romans prevailed and occupied the island.

151
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:04,800
After bristling for 20 years under Roman rule,

152
00:10:04,801 --> 00:10:08,100
rule, Celtic warrior Queen Boudicca led her people

153
00:10:08,200 --> 00:10:09,200
on a bloody revolt.

154
00:10:18,140 --> 00:10:21,040
Spear in hand, Boudicca stormed into battle,

155
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:22,660
riding one of the finest examples

156
00:10:22,760 --> 00:10:27,860
of barbarian technology, the Celtic chariot.

157
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:30,300
The Celtic chariot becomes a very important part

158
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:32,500
of their myth and their legends.

159
00:10:32,501 --> 00:10:37,500
These became very important symbols, not just war weapons,

160
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:41,380
but symbols of greatness, of warrior greatness.

161
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:44,020
Here, I think we've got the lower jaw.

162
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:46,720
In 2001, archaeologists unearthed

163
00:10:46,820 --> 00:10:50,080
a grave containing remnants of a Celtic chariot that would

164
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:52,920
answer a puzzling riddle about its technology.

165
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:54,860
Experts had never been able to figure out

166
00:10:54,960 --> 00:10:58,760
how the chariot's wheels were secured to the axle.

167
00:10:58,761 --> 00:11:02,060
At the site, ground X-ray revealed a crucial clue,

168
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:03,900
a J-shaped pin.

169
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:07,840
And this is the drawing that really...

170
00:11:07,841 --> 00:11:08,541
Yes.

171
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:11,300
The pin intrigued Mike Lodes and Robert Hereford,

172
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:13,600
who were trying to construct an accurate,

173
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:15,340
fully functional Celtic chariot.

174
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:18,160
Their only chance to succeed

175
00:11:18,161 --> 00:11:20,640
was to solve the mystery of the pin's role.

176
00:11:20,980 --> 00:11:23,680
They were these J-shaped linchpins,

177
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:25,500
quite an interesting shape,

178
00:11:26,260 --> 00:11:28,520
and associated with them in the grave

179
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:30,980
was a little iron ring.

180
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:37,180
Now, we puzzled for ages how this might have worked.

181
00:11:37,640 --> 00:11:40,260
The solution came when research revealed

182
00:11:40,261 --> 00:11:43,680
that Celtic wheel makers cut a groove in their chariot axles.

183
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:48,100
So this J-shape actually fits in the slot.

184
00:11:48,101 --> 00:11:54,100
Then this comes around that and ties to the ring,

185
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:56,880
so keeping it in place.

186
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:01,220
The next step in construction was to form the charioteer's

187
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:02,920
platform.

188
00:12:03,020 --> 00:12:08,760
The platform is a latticework, all made

189
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:11,660
from a single strip of rawhide.

190
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:15,380
And that gives tremendous spring.

191
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:17,780
Most chariots in the world, you stand up on them.

192
00:12:17,880 --> 00:12:20,280
Whether it's an Egyptian chariot, an Assyrian chariot,

193
00:12:20,380 --> 00:12:23,480
Roman racing chariot, or a Greek chariot, you stand.

194
00:12:23,580 --> 00:12:26,280
Because not only have you got some suspension

195
00:12:26,281 --> 00:12:30,580
in the platted rawhide platform, but your legs are spring.

196
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:34,620
So you're very much like a surfer riding away.

197
00:12:34,720 --> 00:12:38,020
A Celtic chariot was built for two, an armed warrior who

198
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:41,320
stood and a driver who sat.

199
00:12:41,420 --> 00:12:44,040
By itself, the platform's springy latticework

200
00:12:44,140 --> 00:12:46,840
wouldn't prevent either from being jostled by every bump

201
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:47,940
and rut.

202
00:12:48,040 --> 00:12:50,640
But Lodes believes the Celts incorporated another design

203
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:53,780
element to ensure a smoother ride.

204
00:12:53,781 --> 00:12:57,380
What if these Ys were straps?

205
00:12:57,460 --> 00:12:59,420
Rawhide straps.

206
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:04,260
And then the platform could be slung in these straps.

207
00:13:04,340 --> 00:13:10,100
Now you've got something that's got lateral and forward movement.

208
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:12,620
It's tied off underneath so it's not too extreme,

209
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:17,860
but it just gives a wonderfully suspended platform,

210
00:13:17,940 --> 00:13:21,140
able to cover almost any terrain.

211
00:13:21,141 --> 00:13:26,140
The Celtic chariot suspension no doubt enhanced its performance

212
00:13:26,141 --> 00:13:30,140
on the battlefield, where it may have been used much like today's

213
00:13:30,141 --> 00:13:31,140
armored personnel carriers.

214
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:38,140
The charioteers would take their warriors close to the front

215
00:13:38,141 --> 00:13:40,140
line and drop them off.

216
00:13:40,141 --> 00:13:43,140
The warrior would get off, and this is where he would come in,

217
00:13:43,141 --> 00:13:44,140
and he would fight.

218
00:13:44,141 --> 00:13:45,040
and he would fight.

219
00:13:48,440 --> 00:13:51,540
So after a few minutes, your charioteer

220
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:53,820
would come racing around the line.

221
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:58,520
And still at the gallop, you would get back onto your chariot,

222
00:13:58,620 --> 00:14:01,360
saying goodbye to the enemy, and have a break

223
00:14:01,460 --> 00:14:03,120
while another man was dropped off.

224
00:14:03,220 --> 00:14:07,360
Constant rotation of front line troops.

225
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:11,760
And Queen Boudicca?

226
00:14:11,761 --> 00:14:13,960
We left her leading the Celts in a pivotal battle

227
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:16,900
against the Romans in AD 60.

228
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:20,760
She counted on her superior chariots to overwhelm them.

229
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:23,140
But barbarian technology in this case

230
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:27,280
proved no match for the empire's disciplined troops.

231
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:30,840
And as they gradually made their advance,

232
00:14:30,940 --> 00:14:36,420
Boudicca's army was condensed into a smaller mass.

233
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:38,760
And they got hemmed in and pressed in.

234
00:14:38,860 --> 00:14:40,320
The chariots couldn't maneuver.

235
00:14:40,321 --> 00:14:44,260
The warriors couldn't fight, and it was carnage.

236
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:47,160
And she was wiped out, and her army was wiped out.

237
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:53,860
After the Roman invasion, the Celts

238
00:14:53,960 --> 00:14:56,840
are the settled native population of Britain,

239
00:14:56,940 --> 00:14:58,940
and they gradually become Romanized.

240
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:05,080
The mighty Romans had secured another conquest.

241
00:15:05,081 --> 00:15:10,980
conquest. But across the English Channel,

242
00:15:10,981 --> 00:15:15,480
other barbarian tribes were growing restless.

243
00:15:15,481 --> 00:15:20,320
One wielding a fearsome new weapon would one day threaten to

244
00:15:20,321 --> 00:15:22,640
topple the empire.

245
00:15:31,540 --> 00:15:34,780
By the end of the 4th century AD, Rome was

246
00:15:34,781 --> 00:15:38,880
disintegrating. Civil war and political upheaval

247
00:15:38,881 --> 00:15:43,160
had torn the vast empire in two. Meanwhile, the Germanic barbarian

248
00:15:43,161 --> 00:15:47,360
tribes in the north were being forced toward Rome by a new group

249
00:15:47,361 --> 00:15:53,360
of barbarians that had swept in from the steppes of Asia.

250
00:15:53,361 --> 00:15:56,900
They flooded into Europe on horseback, raiding and killing

251
00:15:56,901 --> 00:16:04,780
with swift and brutal efficiency. They called themselves the Huns.

252
00:16:04,781 --> 00:16:07,020
The Huns soon reached the Danube.

253
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:10,560
On the other side of the river lay the Roman Empire.

254
00:16:10,660 --> 00:16:13,560
The arrival of the Huns was a terrible shock.

255
00:16:13,660 --> 00:16:15,360
It was a shock to the Germanic peoples.

256
00:16:15,460 --> 00:16:18,860
It was a shock to the Romans.

257
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:23,660
They also came with this formidable war technology.

258
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:26,000
The Huns came armed with an odd-looking,

259
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:29,880
yet devastating weapon, the recurve bow.

260
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:33,640
It featured horn-shaped tips and a curved shaft.

261
00:16:33,641 --> 00:16:37,920
Out on the steps, where there are very few trees,

262
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:40,880
there's much less availability of that sort of raw material

263
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:42,920
to make a bow out of.

264
00:16:43,020 --> 00:16:46,460
So they were forced by what was available

265
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:49,320
to develop something else.

266
00:16:49,420 --> 00:16:52,800
And what they do is to take a thin lath of wood

267
00:16:52,900 --> 00:16:54,300
instead of a chunk of tree.

268
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:56,660
They take a thin lath of wood.

269
00:16:56,760 --> 00:16:59,440
This signature Hun weapon was a marked contrast

270
00:16:59,540 --> 00:17:01,340
to the bow most commonly used in Europe.

271
00:17:03,640 --> 00:17:07,640
The Western longbow was carved from a single limb of a tree

272
00:17:07,740 --> 00:17:10,880
and was only as strong as the wood used to produce it.

273
00:17:10,980 --> 00:17:13,720
Its power came from its length and flexibility,

274
00:17:13,820 --> 00:17:15,880
allowing a deep draw.

275
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:19,720
The shorter recurve Hunbow was made up of several layers of wood

276
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:21,900
glued together against the grain.

277
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:25,060
The horn-shaped tips made the bow curve back on itself,

278
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:29,200
producing added torque and a quick snapping action.

279
00:17:29,300 --> 00:17:30,900
But which bow harnessed more power?

280
00:17:33,640 --> 00:17:37,640
using a kind of sophisticated stopwatch called a chronograph.

281
00:17:37,740 --> 00:17:40,140
It's possible to compare the velocity of an arrow shot

282
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:43,280
from each type of bow.

283
00:17:43,380 --> 00:17:46,680
As you can see, there's a sensor at the front here

284
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:48,780
and a sensor at the rear here.

285
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:51,620
And as the arrow passes across the chronograph,

286
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:55,020
switches the timing on as it passes the front one,

287
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:57,300
switches it off again as it passes the rear one.

288
00:17:57,400 --> 00:18:00,140
And so the device will then record the velocity

289
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:01,640
in feet per second.

290
00:18:01,740 --> 00:18:03,160
And as you see, it's showing the display

291
00:18:03,161 --> 00:18:08,583
the display that it's ready. So we'll try the Hunbow first.

292
00:18:08,584 --> 00:18:12,260
107.4, so that's about 75 miles an hour.

293
00:18:12,261 --> 00:18:17,400
You must remember that the bows we're using here today are only about half as powerful as the ones

294
00:18:17,401 --> 00:18:20,634
they were using, so we could translate that up.

295
00:18:20,635 --> 00:18:23,800
The Hunbow might be reaching 100 miles an hour

296
00:18:23,801 --> 00:18:28,522
at the full power. So here we go with the longbow.

297
00:18:28,523 --> 00:18:32,960
99.22 feet per second, that's about 68 miles an

298
00:18:32,961 --> 00:18:36,400
And we can see that we've lost a fair bit of velocity there

299
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:38,460
in proportion to the power of the bow.

300
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:41,800
It shows that the Hun bow, with its faster action,

301
00:18:41,900 --> 00:18:43,500
its more efficient energy storing,

302
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:45,400
its more efficient energy releasing,

303
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:50,140
is punching that arrow out significantly higher velocity.

304
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:54,040
And that's where the difference is going to lie.

305
00:18:54,140 --> 00:18:56,520
When we get to the Hunnic bow, we're

306
00:18:56,620 --> 00:19:00,660
looking at a weapon that could penetrate some, if not much,

307
00:19:00,661 --> 00:19:05,000
of the armor of the individuals that were opposing them.

308
00:19:05,100 --> 00:19:10,060
Certainly, the breastplates or the segmented or scale

309
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:13,400
armors of the Romans could be penetrated

310
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:16,740
because the metal was not thick, the leather underneath

311
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:17,640
was not thick.

312
00:19:22,180 --> 00:19:25,060
Beyond its superior power, the Hunbow

313
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:27,720
held another advantage over the longbow.

314
00:19:27,820 --> 00:19:30,480
It could be fired from horseback with astounding

315
00:19:30,481 --> 00:19:32,480
precision.

316
00:19:32,580 --> 00:19:35,120
Technology is not just for the killing.

317
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:37,680
It's for the frightening aspect.

318
00:19:37,780 --> 00:19:40,220
And a Hun bearing down on somebody with a bolt

319
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:43,060
that he could fire with great accuracy

320
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:45,100
from a very strong force.

321
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:47,200
The man who was likely to run away

322
00:19:47,300 --> 00:19:50,440
was defeated as much as the man who was penetrated by the air.

323
00:19:53,740 --> 00:19:58,100
By AD 430, the Huns were following a new leader,

324
00:19:58,101 --> 00:20:01,740
Attila, the warrior synonymous with the term barbarian.

325
00:20:05,040 --> 00:20:07,980
He commanded thousands of battle-hardened archers,

326
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:10,080
capable of striking with deadly accuracy.

327
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:16,580
Their tactics introduced a new dimension to the battlefield.

328
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:20,320
The advantage for the Huns was simple, hit and run tactics.

329
00:20:20,420 --> 00:20:23,520
Quite simply, being able to, within seconds,

330
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:26,500
get yourself within bow range, within arrow range,

331
00:20:26,501 --> 00:20:31,100
fire off a quiver full of arrows, 20, 30, 40 arrows even,

332
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:32,940
at an enemy which cannot reach you,

333
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:36,740
and then disappearing again to feel your quiver.

334
00:20:36,840 --> 00:20:40,340
The Hun bow was tailor-made for this hit and run style.

335
00:20:40,440 --> 00:20:43,520
Unlike the long bow, the short and powerful Hun bow

336
00:20:43,620 --> 00:20:46,980
is very maneuverable, allowing it to be used from both sides

337
00:20:47,080 --> 00:20:50,420
of the horse and also giving a great range of fire.

338
00:20:53,920 --> 00:20:55,860
Historians still debate whether or not

339
00:20:55,861 --> 00:20:59,600
used stirrups to help them keep their balance.

340
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:02,100
If they did, their prowess on the battlefield

341
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:05,200
could have only been enhanced.

342
00:21:05,300 --> 00:21:09,500
The stirrup is vital for combat on horseback,

343
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:11,200
absolutely vital.

344
00:21:11,300 --> 00:21:15,520
This allows you to distribute your weight over a much greater

345
00:21:15,620 --> 00:21:18,220
area on the horse's back, and it creates, effectively,

346
00:21:18,320 --> 00:21:24,620
a three-point fighting platform, your feet and your butt.

347
00:21:24,621 --> 00:21:26,180
And this enables you, with a bow,

348
00:21:26,280 --> 00:21:29,620
to lean right forward, over to the side, and back.

349
00:21:39,160 --> 00:21:41,900
By 440, Attila's army had thundered

350
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:43,900
through a wide swath of southern Europe.

351
00:21:48,540 --> 00:21:51,740
But as they moved west, it was slowed by an obstacle

352
00:21:51,741 --> 00:21:55,340
never confronted on their native steps.

353
00:21:55,440 --> 00:21:58,240
The further into the forest and woodland they came,

354
00:21:58,340 --> 00:22:01,480
the less could they maneuver their horse archers

355
00:22:01,580 --> 00:22:04,380
at high speed.

356
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:06,160
The unaccustomed terrain wasn't

357
00:22:06,260 --> 00:22:08,260
the Huns' only problem.

358
00:22:08,360 --> 00:22:12,100
In 451 in Gaul, they clashed with the combined might

359
00:22:12,200 --> 00:22:14,860
of Roman legions and other barbarian tribes.

360
00:22:14,960 --> 00:22:19,240
The fearsome Huns fell at last.

361
00:22:19,241 --> 00:22:23,240
One of the barbarian tribes that helped defeat them was the

362
00:22:23,241 --> 00:22:25,880
Goths. They were destined to be the first

363
00:22:25,881 --> 00:22:29,240
barbarians to storm through the gates of Rome.

364
00:22:35,240 --> 00:22:38,200
When the marauding Huns tore through Europe in the 4th

365
00:22:38,201 --> 00:22:44,280
century, several barbarian tribes fled in their wake.

366
00:22:44,281 --> 00:22:48,280
One of the displaced tribes was the Goths.

367
00:22:48,281 --> 00:22:51,980
Driven to the border of the Roman Empire in AD 376,

368
00:22:51,981 --> 00:22:54,920
the Goths had no choice but to cut a deal with imperial

369
00:22:54,921 --> 00:22:58,920
officials. In exchange for asylum in Roman

370
00:22:58,921 --> 00:23:02,120
territory, they agreed to fight for the declining and divided

371
00:23:02,121 --> 00:23:06,540
empire. Under Rome's thumb, these

372
00:23:06,541 --> 00:23:09,540
barbarians proved they had more to offer than just military

373
00:23:09,541 --> 00:23:12,980
manpower. The Goths were renowned

374
00:23:12,981 --> 00:23:18,280
metallurgists who crafted intricate and ornate jewelry.

375
00:23:18,281 --> 00:23:19,620
And the coins they made reflected

376
00:23:19,621 --> 00:23:21,980
the same masterful workmanship they used

377
00:23:21,981 --> 00:23:23,180
to produce their weaponry.

378
00:23:26,880 --> 00:23:29,720
They were particularly good at refining silver.

379
00:23:29,721 --> 00:23:33,500
So their coins had a value above what most people would

380
00:23:33,501 --> 00:23:36,000
have expected for silver coins at their time.

381
00:23:36,001 --> 00:23:40,840
The coins were about 98% pure silver.

382
00:23:40,841 --> 00:23:42,840
Working from a sheet of silver,

383
00:23:42,841 --> 00:23:45,680
goth coin makers began by striking round blanks

384
00:23:45,681 --> 00:23:48,280
with a hole punch.

385
00:23:48,281 --> 00:23:51,280
Then you need a pair of dyes.

386
00:23:51,281 --> 00:23:56,280
These are struck and made with tiny punches.

387
00:23:56,281 --> 00:24:02,280
Here the expression, to make money, hand over fist.

388
00:24:04,280 --> 00:24:08,280
And the coin is worth about $50.

389
00:24:08,281 --> 00:24:11,280
On the back of the coin is a small cross.

390
00:24:11,281 --> 00:24:14,280
The way that you get small occurrences

391
00:24:14,281 --> 00:24:18,980
is by the simple procedure of using the cutting guide,

392
00:24:19,080 --> 00:24:21,380
the small cross in the middle of the coin,

393
00:24:21,480 --> 00:24:26,620
so that you cut the coin in half, achieving two half pennies.

394
00:24:26,720 --> 00:24:29,720
While Goth coins have endured for centuries,

395
00:24:29,820 --> 00:24:31,940
few Goth weapons have survived.

396
00:24:32,040 --> 00:24:33,940
But scholars believe we can understand

397
00:24:34,040 --> 00:24:38,540
what the Goths wielded in battle by examining Roman weaponry,

398
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:41,880
as the technology of the two cultures eventually merged.

399
00:24:41,881 --> 00:24:44,780
The auxiliary units of the Roman army included people

400
00:24:44,880 --> 00:24:47,580
who were drawn from, quote, barbarian lands,

401
00:24:47,680 --> 00:24:49,680
and the customs that they brought with them,

402
00:24:49,780 --> 00:24:51,920
whether it was for decorating their weaponry

403
00:24:52,020 --> 00:24:53,820
or, indeed, the weapons themselves.

404
00:24:53,920 --> 00:24:56,120
Well, sometimes there's not very much difference, therefore,

405
00:24:56,220 --> 00:24:58,400
between the weapons that the Romans were using

406
00:24:58,500 --> 00:25:01,660
and the weapons that they were barbarians were using.

407
00:25:01,760 --> 00:25:04,400
Just as Roman arms makers copied the Goths,

408
00:25:04,500 --> 00:25:07,140
the Goths copied the Romans.

409
00:25:07,240 --> 00:25:10,000
They have adopted a lot of the ways of the Roman people,

410
00:25:10,001 --> 00:25:12,340
of Roman people, particularly armor.

411
00:25:12,440 --> 00:25:14,340
We have on the top here a riveted helmet.

412
00:25:14,440 --> 00:25:17,140
The 4th century saw a change in the way helmets are made.

413
00:25:17,240 --> 00:25:19,340
They're often made of segments, which are then riveted together

414
00:25:19,440 --> 00:25:20,440
into a rigid armor.

415
00:25:20,540 --> 00:25:22,740
We've got the crest across the top here to protect against blows

416
00:25:22,840 --> 00:25:27,060
coming down from the side, and a movable neck piece at the back,

417
00:25:27,160 --> 00:25:29,960
and the nasal guard there, obviously protecting

418
00:25:30,060 --> 00:25:32,060
those from chops.

419
00:25:32,160 --> 00:25:34,560
Goth soldiers were clad in mail,

420
00:25:34,660 --> 00:25:37,260
the pliable metal body armor used in the Roman army

421
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:40,000
before the birth of Christ.

422
00:25:40,001 --> 00:25:45,440
Mail itself is just made from rings, which are, in this case,

423
00:25:45,540 --> 00:25:49,340
butted together, forming a very flexible suit of armor,

424
00:25:49,440 --> 00:25:53,740
which sword slipes and attacks and cuts just slide clean off

425
00:25:53,840 --> 00:25:54,640
of.

426
00:25:54,740 --> 00:25:57,660
Later on, we see an increasing amount of riveted mail.

427
00:25:57,760 --> 00:25:59,660
Because the problem with this is a spear or an arrow

428
00:25:59,760 --> 00:26:01,260
can push the rings apart.

429
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:03,660
It's fine on a cut, on a piercing action,

430
00:26:03,760 --> 00:26:07,860
it can go clean through and push them apart.

431
00:26:07,861 --> 00:26:11,360
As the decaying Roman Empire became increasingly fragmented,

432
00:26:11,460 --> 00:26:14,660
so too did the fields of battle.

433
00:26:14,760 --> 00:26:17,100
The Imperial Legion scattered, trying

434
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:21,200
to hold the far-flung territories together.

435
00:26:21,300 --> 00:26:25,920
And the scope of military engagements shrank.

436
00:26:26,020 --> 00:26:28,220
Arms makers responded with weapons tailored

437
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:30,120
for a more mobile soldier.

438
00:26:30,220 --> 00:26:32,520
There seems to be, at least according to the records

439
00:26:32,620 --> 00:26:34,560
and according to some of the myths that come around,

440
00:26:34,561 --> 00:26:39,000
a lot more of individual fighting, one-on-one type of fighting.

441
00:26:39,100 --> 00:26:41,760
Combat at closer quarters compelled Roman and Goth

442
00:26:41,860 --> 00:26:45,100
ironsmiths to produce fewer slashing type swords

443
00:26:45,200 --> 00:26:47,100
and make more practical stabbing weapons.

444
00:26:50,600 --> 00:26:52,620
A lot of the work we think at this time

445
00:26:52,720 --> 00:26:55,920
was done more with the tip than it was with most of the blade.

446
00:26:56,020 --> 00:26:58,020
Remember, even a blade made for fighting

447
00:26:58,120 --> 00:27:00,060
is still a considerable investment.

448
00:27:00,160 --> 00:27:02,320
So if you can use this more like a spear

449
00:27:02,420 --> 00:27:04,320
and thrust it into your enemy quickly,

450
00:27:04,321 --> 00:27:06,760
You can save the blade and subsequent work and so on

451
00:27:06,761 --> 00:27:08,000
that had to be done to it.

452
00:27:08,001 --> 00:27:10,600
The effects of this going into a person are quite shocking.

453
00:27:10,601 --> 00:27:12,720
You see you've got the nerves and so on on the skin.

454
00:27:12,721 --> 00:27:14,960
Then you go into deep tissue or organs.

455
00:27:14,961 --> 00:27:17,160
You're going to cause internal bleeding.

456
00:27:17,161 --> 00:27:19,760
You're going to take bits of fabric in there with you.

457
00:27:19,761 --> 00:27:21,400
It's just going to be a right mess.

458
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,840
The best way to kill him is to get in here and push up,

459
00:27:26,841 --> 00:27:28,280
because then you're going to get all the organs.

460
00:27:28,281 --> 00:27:30,640
You can't get past the ribcage.

461
00:27:30,641 --> 00:27:33,920
Only if you get a very slight angle, you get it in there.

462
00:27:33,921 --> 00:27:36,720
But we know if you actually come up underneath,

463
00:27:36,721 --> 00:27:38,520
you'll damage all his organs.

464
00:27:38,521 --> 00:27:41,320
And we only know that you only need to go in that far

465
00:27:41,321 --> 00:27:43,720
to actually damage any of his organs.

466
00:27:43,721 --> 00:27:47,320
So the thrust upwards would be the killer thrust.

467
00:27:47,321 --> 00:27:50,920
Also the thrust down here, the main artery here.

468
00:27:50,921 --> 00:27:54,120
So you're trying to get in here and you're trying to get out there.

469
00:27:54,121 --> 00:27:56,320
And under his arm, just here.

470
00:27:57,320 --> 00:28:01,320
You get in there an inch or so, there's a main artery and he's dead.

471
00:28:01,321 --> 00:28:05,320
As the Goths and Romans modified their weaponry,

472
00:28:05,420 --> 00:28:08,820
their uneasy alliance deteriorated.

473
00:28:08,920 --> 00:28:13,060
In 377, just one year after sealing their pact with Rome,

474
00:28:13,160 --> 00:28:15,560
the Goths suffered a devastating famine,

475
00:28:15,660 --> 00:28:17,560
and Rome let them starve.

476
00:28:20,180 --> 00:28:21,980
The Goths revolted.

477
00:28:24,180 --> 00:28:27,280
Unleashing their fury against the imperial army,

478
00:28:27,380 --> 00:28:31,320
years of bloody conflict rocked the weakening empire.

479
00:28:31,321 --> 00:28:40,080
So we are fourth century goth versus Roman

480
00:28:40,081 --> 00:28:54,081
And here we have the male my vest protecting me against the shot I've moved in close so the edge just moves out against me

481
00:29:01,320 --> 00:29:05,320
By 395, the Goths were primed to deliver the death blow.

482
00:29:05,420 --> 00:29:08,420
Their new king, Alaric, marched his warriors

483
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:12,460
toward the imperial capital.

484
00:29:12,560 --> 00:29:15,760
In 410, he and the Goths achieved the unthinkable.

485
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:22,280
They swiped the jewel of the Roman Empire.

486
00:29:22,380 --> 00:29:25,080
They take the city of Rome, but they do it essentially

487
00:29:25,180 --> 00:29:27,080
by cutting off the supplies of Rome.

488
00:29:27,180 --> 00:29:30,180
And therefore, it was quite easy to starve it into submission

489
00:29:30,181 --> 00:29:32,320
And eventually, Rome, they open the gates,

490
00:29:32,420 --> 00:29:34,420
and Rome is sacked for the first time.

491
00:29:39,120 --> 00:29:43,420
But occupying the city of Rome wasn't Alaric's ultimate goal.

492
00:29:43,520 --> 00:29:48,340
He wanted a Goth homeland somewhere within the empire.

493
00:29:48,440 --> 00:29:50,840
10 years after his shocking victory,

494
00:29:50,940 --> 00:29:54,580
officials of the dying empire finally granted him the first

495
00:29:54,680 --> 00:29:58,480
barbarian kingdom in southern Gaul.

496
00:29:58,481 --> 00:30:00,580
It was there that they encountered another tribe

497
00:30:00,680 --> 00:30:04,220
of barbarians, the Franks.

498
00:30:04,320 --> 00:30:07,820
Its warriors were masters of a small, deadly throwing axe

499
00:30:07,920 --> 00:30:09,720
called the Francisca.

500
00:30:09,820 --> 00:30:12,420
They were weapons that could be launched from afar,

501
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:15,120
like a javelin, but with a lot more power,

502
00:30:15,240 --> 00:30:18,240
and a power that was used by the fact

503
00:30:18,340 --> 00:30:22,240
that the axe turned on its axis as it was being thrown.

504
00:30:22,340 --> 00:30:25,240
The first thing that this weapon is going to be able to do,

505
00:30:25,340 --> 00:30:27,840
it'll distract the opponent, at least.

506
00:30:27,841 --> 00:30:30,080
take his eye off the fighting.

507
00:30:30,180 --> 00:30:34,240
If it gets through, even if it doesn't break the male shirt,

508
00:30:34,340 --> 00:30:35,780
it will injure him.

509
00:30:35,880 --> 00:30:39,880
It will stop him from continuing in the battle.

510
00:30:39,980 --> 00:30:43,220
If you're lucky, if you're good, hit him in the head,

511
00:30:43,320 --> 00:30:47,300
hit him in the neck, break his head in two, cut it clean off.

512
00:30:47,400 --> 00:30:51,400
Really powerful, really brutal weapon.

513
00:30:51,500 --> 00:30:53,500
Another strategic plus of the Francisco

514
00:30:53,600 --> 00:30:56,240
was its unpredictability.

515
00:30:56,241 --> 00:30:59,280
If you threw the axe to the ground at the proper angle,

516
00:30:59,380 --> 00:31:01,480
its lethal blade would ricochet wildly

517
00:31:01,580 --> 00:31:03,480
into the onrushing enemy.

518
00:31:03,580 --> 00:31:07,980
It describes a kind of erratic loop through the air, which,

519
00:31:08,080 --> 00:31:10,980
when you're a defender, you're getting this kind of peculiar

520
00:31:11,080 --> 00:31:13,000
axe shape coming through.

521
00:31:13,100 --> 00:31:16,300
It's very hard to guess where it's going to hit you.

522
00:31:16,400 --> 00:31:19,300
Don't forget, this isn't just being thrown and then ignored.

523
00:31:19,400 --> 00:31:21,900
This is being followed by the angry man with it,

524
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:25,000
who's now drawing another weapon to follow that axe.

525
00:31:25,001 --> 00:31:26,500
that axe.

526
00:31:26,600 --> 00:31:28,500
The Francisca had an S-shaped curve

527
00:31:28,600 --> 00:31:31,840
at the top of its iron head, and the lower edge of the blade

528
00:31:31,940 --> 00:31:34,500
was rounded inward.

529
00:31:34,600 --> 00:31:37,740
The center of the axe head formed an angle of approximately 90

530
00:31:37,840 --> 00:31:41,240
to 115 degrees to the handle.

531
00:31:41,340 --> 00:31:44,120
Most Francisca axe heads weighed between a half pound

532
00:31:44,220 --> 00:31:45,980
and three pounds, and their length

533
00:31:46,080 --> 00:31:49,820
varied between 5 and 12 inches.

534
00:31:49,920 --> 00:31:51,520
Look at the shape of the head on this.

535
00:31:51,620 --> 00:31:54,060
Look how much weight there is, all

536
00:31:54,061 --> 00:31:55,820
all at the head of a haft.

537
00:31:55,821 --> 00:31:58,380
What that means is it's very heavy up here.

538
00:31:58,381 --> 00:32:02,620
That way, even if you miss with the edge,

539
00:32:02,621 --> 00:32:04,820
you've still got a lot of power, a lot of weight there,

540
00:32:04,821 --> 00:32:06,020
in a very small reach.

541
00:32:06,021 --> 00:32:08,620
So you can come in very, very fast indeed.

542
00:32:13,580 --> 00:32:15,500
Look at that.

543
00:32:15,501 --> 00:32:17,820
Straight through.

544
00:32:17,821 --> 00:32:19,220
Man's now without a shield.

545
00:32:19,221 --> 00:32:21,180
The next shot, that's him dead.

546
00:32:24,060 --> 00:32:27,260
Wielding the Francisca, the Franks sliced through every

547
00:32:27,261 --> 00:32:30,860
barbarian tribe that challenged them, including the Goths.

548
00:32:30,861 --> 00:32:34,860
By the 6th century AD, all of Gaul was theirs.

549
00:32:34,861 --> 00:32:38,860
The Franks renamed the region, inspired by the lethal Francisca

550
00:32:38,861 --> 00:32:42,460
that helped them seize it. They called it Francia.

551
00:32:42,461 --> 00:32:48,460
Today, it's France. Across the English Channel,

552
00:32:48,461 --> 00:32:52,060
yet another barbarian tribe had stormed the shores of Britain,

553
00:32:52,061 --> 00:32:55,060
and they were making waves with their own battle technology.

554
00:33:04,500 --> 00:33:08,060
After the collapse of the Roman Empire in AD 410,

555
00:33:08,061 --> 00:33:10,820
its soldiers occupying Britain pulled out.

556
00:33:10,821 --> 00:33:14,560
Their withdrawal created a power vacuum.

557
00:33:14,561 --> 00:33:16,560
And as word spread across Europe,

558
00:33:16,561 --> 00:33:19,560
a number of barbarian tribes made the treacherous sea journey

559
00:33:19,561 --> 00:33:23,060
across the English Channel to claim their piece of the island.

560
00:33:26,060 --> 00:33:29,800
For years, the competing tribes battled for supremacy,

561
00:33:29,900 --> 00:33:34,640
until one, the Anglo-Saxons, emerged victorious.

562
00:33:34,740 --> 00:33:36,980
In the new British kingdom they established,

563
00:33:37,080 --> 00:33:40,320
their culture and religious beliefs prevailed.

564
00:33:40,420 --> 00:33:46,680
They believed in a large group of gods who all had power.

565
00:33:46,681 --> 00:33:50,320
And they also seemed to have believed that you needed to be

566
00:33:50,420 --> 00:33:54,120
buried with things to take with you to the afterlife.

567
00:33:54,220 --> 00:33:57,160
In 1939, archaeologists unearthed

568
00:33:57,260 --> 00:34:00,920
Anglo-Saxon burial mounds in the town of Sutton Hoo.

569
00:34:01,020 --> 00:34:03,760
One of the graves contained the remains of a warrior next to

570
00:34:03,860 --> 00:34:05,960
the remnants of a ship.

571
00:34:06,060 --> 00:34:08,700
The discovery shed vivid new light on the militaristic

572
00:34:08,800 --> 00:34:11,740
culture he inhabited.

573
00:34:11,840 --> 00:34:15,980
They saw not just a warrior of great renown,

574
00:34:15,981 --> 00:34:19,480
but a warrior of great renown whose entire accoutrement

575
00:34:19,580 --> 00:34:20,620
was still there.

576
00:34:20,720 --> 00:34:23,560
In fact, the rivets from the boat were still there,

577
00:34:23,660 --> 00:34:26,220
so they could actually see how large the boat was

578
00:34:26,320 --> 00:34:28,120
that he had been buried in.

579
00:34:28,220 --> 00:34:30,020
Sutton Hoo yielded many weapons

580
00:34:30,120 --> 00:34:32,300
containing Iron Age medals.

581
00:34:32,400 --> 00:34:35,640
Gold wire decorated the warrior's sword.

582
00:34:35,740 --> 00:34:40,100
Bronze panel is coated with tin, lined his steel helmet.

583
00:34:40,200 --> 00:34:43,080
And an iron boss used to protect his hand

584
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:47,220
his hand capped his spectacular shield, all proof of his

585
00:34:47,221 --> 00:34:52,420
culture's mastery of metallurgy. When the Anglo-Saxons

586
00:34:52,421 --> 00:34:55,520
weren't duking it out on the battlefield, they lived in small

587
00:34:55,521 --> 00:34:59,320
villages scattered across England. In the center of each

588
00:34:59,321 --> 00:35:02,940
village was the Mead Hall, an oblong structure where the lord

589
00:35:02,941 --> 00:35:08,140
of the tribe conducted business and tipped a few flagons.

590
00:35:08,141 --> 00:35:11,740
Members of a reenactment group called Regia Anglorum have

591
00:35:11,741 --> 00:35:16,740
institution of Anglo-Saxon culture. Behind me is a

592
00:35:16,741 --> 00:35:19,740
reconstruction of an Anglo-Saxon Mead Hall.

593
00:35:19,741 --> 00:35:23,740
An Anglo-Saxon Mead Hall is a place where the Lord gathers his

594
00:35:23,741 --> 00:35:29,740
taxes, entertains guests, and in defensive crisis, gathers his

595
00:35:29,741 --> 00:35:35,740
troops either to attack or to defend. Mead was a honey-sweetened

596
00:35:35,741 --> 00:35:38,740
alcoholic beverage that the Saxons drank to break the chill

597
00:35:38,741 --> 00:35:41,040
the chill of the harsh English winters.

598
00:35:41,140 --> 00:35:44,180
It filled every cup during their pre-war rituals.

599
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:46,740
The drinking in the Mead Hall, the men drank together

600
00:35:46,840 --> 00:35:49,380
and swore pledges to each other that when the time came

601
00:35:49,480 --> 00:35:51,980
and the enemy came against them with spear and with shield,

602
00:35:52,080 --> 00:35:54,980
with point and edge, that they would be able to stand together

603
00:35:55,080 --> 00:35:57,200
in the Shield War and fight together.

604
00:35:57,300 --> 00:35:59,300
The men that drank together fought together

605
00:35:59,400 --> 00:36:02,300
because they had said they would.

606
00:36:02,400 --> 00:36:05,800
After the drinking came the call to arms.

607
00:36:05,801 --> 00:36:10,600
On the walls, the weapons of the Lord and his warriors.

608
00:36:10,700 --> 00:36:13,800
We have the lightweight throwing javelin

609
00:36:13,900 --> 00:36:15,840
discharged at the enemy.

610
00:36:15,940 --> 00:36:20,580
The fighting spear, with its sharp edge and point,

611
00:36:20,680 --> 00:36:25,980
used overarm in conjunction with a shield.

612
00:36:26,080 --> 00:36:28,360
The fighting spear had been a battlefield mainstay

613
00:36:28,460 --> 00:36:30,760
in Britain since the Celts first thumbed their noses

614
00:36:30,860 --> 00:36:32,760
at the Romans.

615
00:36:32,761 --> 00:36:34,760
You could always count on it to make its point.

616
00:36:38,760 --> 00:36:40,560
Now, to kill him from here, I'll probably

617
00:36:40,660 --> 00:36:42,900
look to get a spear into him as hard as I can,

618
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:44,000
just keep going at him.

619
00:36:44,100 --> 00:36:46,000
I put the shield down, protect my body.

620
00:36:46,100 --> 00:36:47,400
After all, he is a trained killer

621
00:36:47,500 --> 00:36:49,000
and could cause me all sorts of mischief.

622
00:36:49,100 --> 00:36:51,320
And then a few thrusts to his face,

623
00:36:51,420 --> 00:36:53,720
perhaps even after that, a quick smash with the shield

624
00:36:53,820 --> 00:36:56,820
as I move on to continue the fight.

625
00:36:56,920 --> 00:36:59,120
One difference between the Anglo-Saxon spears

626
00:36:59,220 --> 00:37:02,760
and earlier models was its spear point.

627
00:37:02,761 --> 00:37:05,400
Saxon metal workers crafted it to inflict deeper

628
00:37:05,500 --> 00:37:08,200
and deadlier wounds.

629
00:37:08,300 --> 00:37:11,160
If you look at this wonderfully made spear point,

630
00:37:11,260 --> 00:37:13,500
we recognize it's thinner at the point.

631
00:37:13,600 --> 00:37:16,100
It's even still sharp to a certain extent.

632
00:37:16,200 --> 00:37:20,420
This will penetrate the body and penetrate quite far

633
00:37:20,520 --> 00:37:23,820
into the body, the point where it probably won't go through,

634
00:37:23,920 --> 00:37:25,420
but will get stuck there.

635
00:37:25,520 --> 00:37:27,920
The individual is probably having to pull with all his might

636
00:37:28,020 --> 00:37:31,460
to get it out of the body as the flesh comes around this

637
00:37:31,461 --> 00:37:36,100
around this and circles around the smaller socket.

638
00:37:36,200 --> 00:37:37,900
The most distinctive Saxon weapon

639
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:42,500
was a single-edged knife called the sea axe.

640
00:37:42,600 --> 00:37:46,040
The sea axe, or knife, the weapon for which

641
00:37:46,140 --> 00:37:50,240
the Saxons were named, a utility weapon, a general purpose

642
00:37:50,340 --> 00:37:54,480
weapon, a weapon of war.

643
00:37:54,580 --> 00:37:56,560
The Anglo-Saxons repelled every invader

644
00:37:56,660 --> 00:37:57,760
that dared to challenge them.

645
00:38:01,460 --> 00:38:04,460
Eventually, their pagan culture fell,

646
00:38:04,560 --> 00:38:06,300
not at the point of an enemy's sword,

647
00:38:06,400 --> 00:38:08,260
but as Christianity transformed Britain

648
00:38:08,360 --> 00:38:11,200
starting in the seventh century.

649
00:38:11,300 --> 00:38:13,900
Still, their legacy endures in the form

650
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:17,600
of the international language they left behind.

651
00:38:17,700 --> 00:38:19,480
They were pretty good at what they did as far

652
00:38:19,580 --> 00:38:20,720
as weapons were concerned.

653
00:38:20,820 --> 00:38:21,520
Here we all are.

654
00:38:21,620 --> 00:38:22,920
We speak English.

655
00:38:23,020 --> 00:38:24,720
What better indication do you need of the fact

656
00:38:24,820 --> 00:38:25,820
that they were successful?

657
00:38:31,460 --> 00:38:35,260
For nearly 1,500 years, the barbarian cultures had slashed

658
00:38:35,360 --> 00:38:38,560
their way into history.

659
00:38:38,660 --> 00:38:41,800
Battle after battle, they proved their weaponry was as effective

660
00:38:41,900 --> 00:38:45,400
as that of any foe.

661
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:47,700
But what was the best weapon of the barbarian age?

662
00:38:51,780 --> 00:38:57,020
Surprisingly, the creators of a video game may have the answer.

663
00:39:01,460 --> 00:39:08,460
Imagine you're a barbarian. That'll be easier for some compared

664
00:39:08,461 --> 00:39:12,460
to others. What barbarian weapon would you

665
00:39:12,461 --> 00:39:18,460
carry into battle? Which was deadliest? Historians can only

666
00:39:18,461 --> 00:39:23,460
speculate, but the game designers at the Creative Assembly in

667
00:39:23,461 --> 00:39:26,460
England have devised a formula that rates the Iron Age's most

668
00:39:26,461 --> 00:39:30,460
lethal weapons against the defenses used to counter them.

669
00:39:30,461 --> 00:39:33,360
them.

670
00:39:33,460 --> 00:39:37,500
The results play out as bloody good fun in the animated game

671
00:39:37,600 --> 00:39:38,700
Barbarian Invasion.

672
00:39:43,800 --> 00:39:46,900
We created a huge spreadsheet.

673
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:49,620
And with that, we actually kind of started

674
00:39:49,720 --> 00:39:52,220
to rank those weapons and shields compared to each other

675
00:39:52,320 --> 00:39:53,420
and the armor that they used.

676
00:39:53,520 --> 00:39:55,460
And then also, on top of that, we

677
00:39:55,560 --> 00:39:57,660
layered the actual skill of the people who

678
00:39:57,760 --> 00:39:59,620
were using those weapons and armor.

679
00:39:59,621 --> 00:40:04,100
And the result of that is that we ended up with a very, very

680
00:40:04,101 --> 00:40:07,360
complex network of connections.

681
00:40:07,361 --> 00:40:11,000
For each weapon, designers calculated an attack value

682
00:40:11,001 --> 00:40:14,460
based on its capacity to penetrate defenses.

683
00:40:14,461 --> 00:40:18,240
The very high attack rating means that it's more likely,

684
00:40:18,241 --> 00:40:21,900
when it strikes somebody, to knock them over and certainly

685
00:40:21,901 --> 00:40:24,880
to kill them than any other type of sword.

686
00:40:24,881 --> 00:40:28,020
Take swords, for example.

687
00:40:28,021 --> 00:40:30,860
Designers rated the Roman gladius highly because it could

688
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:33,460
cut effectively into armor.

689
00:40:33,560 --> 00:40:36,260
But they ranked the slashing Celtic sword even higher

690
00:40:36,360 --> 00:40:38,060
because of its added length.

691
00:40:38,160 --> 00:40:40,460
A Celtic sword could inflict terrible damage.

692
00:40:40,560 --> 00:40:44,140
Swung by a strong man, it would probably

693
00:40:44,240 --> 00:40:48,440
take your head clean off, or it would split your head open.

694
00:40:48,540 --> 00:40:50,080
While no other sword ranked higher

695
00:40:50,180 --> 00:40:53,380
than that of the Celts, the Hun bow

696
00:40:53,480 --> 00:40:56,780
emerged as one of the game's deadliest overall weapons.

697
00:40:56,781 --> 00:40:59,020
It would be much more likely to kill a man,

698
00:40:59,021 --> 00:41:03,960
and therefore more likely as well to knock the person over,

699
00:41:04,140 --> 00:41:06,620
because obviously the strength of the bow,

700
00:41:06,840 --> 00:41:08,300
the killing power of the bow,

701
00:41:08,460 --> 00:41:11,820
relates to its velocity and its penetrated power.

702
00:41:12,260 --> 00:41:16,280
A Hunnish bow would probably punch straight through most Roman armor.

703
00:41:17,100 --> 00:41:18,720
It's a very efficient weapon.

704
00:41:19,180 --> 00:41:20,680
It's got a good pull on it.

705
00:41:20,760 --> 00:41:24,540
It's got the kind of pull that you would expect from a modern sports bow.

706
00:41:24,541 --> 00:41:27,780
Score one for Attila's gang.

707
00:41:27,880 --> 00:41:30,940
But the Hunbow lost points for a surprising reason.

708
00:41:33,020 --> 00:41:35,920
Its diminished performance in the rain.

709
00:41:36,020 --> 00:41:38,620
The Hunbow was generally more vulnerable in the rain

710
00:41:38,720 --> 00:41:40,460
because it was made of composite materials.

711
00:41:40,560 --> 00:41:45,360
And so it wasn't as sturdy as a Western-style bow,

712
00:41:45,460 --> 00:41:47,800
which was a single piece of wood.

713
00:41:47,900 --> 00:41:49,960
It's made of several layers of material.

714
00:41:50,060 --> 00:41:51,960
When it gets damp, the glue starts giving way

715
00:41:52,060 --> 00:41:53,800
that's holding the different layers together.

716
00:41:53,801 --> 00:41:55,300
it actually starts delaminating.

717
00:41:58,300 --> 00:42:00,600
On a rainy day, trash your honbo

718
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:03,140
and grab what designers deemed was perhaps the deadliest

719
00:42:03,240 --> 00:42:06,640
weapon of all, despite its small size, the Francisca

720
00:42:06,740 --> 00:42:09,140
throwing axe.

721
00:42:09,240 --> 00:42:11,560
With the Francisca, when it hits a man,

722
00:42:11,660 --> 00:42:18,660
we actually decided that it should have two types of value,

723
00:42:18,760 --> 00:42:23,560
bashing value, and penetration.

724
00:42:23,561 --> 00:42:27,800
I can actually throw my weapon at the enemy,

725
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:30,200
and that's going to cause them some harm.

726
00:42:30,300 --> 00:42:31,960
Then when it comes into close combat,

727
00:42:32,060 --> 00:42:34,800
I've also got the ability to block his blows.

728
00:42:34,900 --> 00:42:37,540
I've got the ability to fight hand to hand

729
00:42:37,640 --> 00:42:39,180
with somebody with a sword.

730
00:42:39,280 --> 00:42:42,540
And at the same time, I can cause some pretty lethal wounds.

731
00:42:45,680 --> 00:42:47,960
We've saddled the barbarians with a name that

732
00:42:48,060 --> 00:42:50,860
disparages their cultures and their contributions.

733
00:42:50,861 --> 00:42:56,200
contributions. But there's no denying the

734
00:42:56,201 --> 00:42:58,200
inventiveness of their weapons designers.

735
00:43:01,600 --> 00:43:05,280
Their ingenious arsenals powered the march of armies across Europe

736
00:43:05,281 --> 00:43:08,940
for century after century in battles that helped determine

737
00:43:08,941 --> 00:43:16,160
the course of history. Not bad for a bunch of barbarians.

